aring get results isn't fully informative. Could compare internal data keys against reference (using the same internal shape) — our data is OrderedDict key→(v,t). For fuzz, compare set of live (non-expired) keys and their values via get, plus internal data keys? Internal key set should equal live keys after operations that purge... Actually after operations, our data may still contain some expired keys if no put/get touched them? Wait, put purges all expired on each put, and get removes the accessed one. Suppose sequence: put a, advance, get missing (doesn't purge), then data still has expired a while reference purges lazily... comparing internal sets would be unfair. Compare observable gets and that expired entries don't block capacity: we can compare get() results for all keys and also verify that after any put, data contains no expired keys (invariant of our impl) — but fuzz against reference should compare behavior, plus a \"capacity\" property: after put, len(data) <= max(capacity, ...) and all entries live.\n\nSimplest: fuzz operations (put/get with random keys/times), after each op, for every key in the universe assert our get == reference get (both return default for expired); also assert len(data) <= capacity when capacity>0 and no expired entries present after put. Reference get: if key live → value (and refresh LRU), else default. Since get mutates recency, reference must mirror.\n\nModel:\n```python\nclass Ref:\n    def __init__(self, cap, ttl):\n        self.cap=cap; self.ttl=ttl; self.entries={}  # k: [v, t0, last]\n        self.seq=0\n    def put(self,k,v,now):\n        if self.cap<=0: return\n        self.entries.pop(k,None)\n        # evict expired first (all), then LRU while full\n        for kk in [kk for kk,(vv,t0,last) in self.entries.items() if now-t0>=self.ttl]:\n            del self.entries[kk]\n        while len(self.entries)>=self.cap:\n            victim=min(self.entries, key=lambda kk: self.entries[kk][2])\n            del self.entries[vi