{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Performance is good. Now verify the scratch tests fail against baseline (to confirm tests are meaningful). Create a baseline test runner that imports from /dev/shm/baseline. My scratch test hardcodes sys.path.insert for repo. I can change the scratch test to check an env var or copy the test and sed the path. Let me create a copy in /dev/shm with the path pointing to /dev/shm/baseline and run — expect failures.\n\nActually simpler: run python with PYTHONPATH=/dev/shm/baseline and modify the import? The scratch test explicitly inserts repo path at position 0, so repo wins. I can create a modified copy via sed replacing the path. Let me do it with shell sed... but instructions say prefer dedicated tools over sed for editing files. This is a throwaway scratch file in /dev/shm; using sed is acceptable for a one-off? The guidance says use edit_file over sed for editing files. I'll just write a small baseline check script instead, testing a few key behaviors against baseline cache.\n\nActually, simplest: use edit_file on the /dev/shm copy? I'd need to copy first. Let me just write a small script that loads baseline cache.py via importlib with a custom module name. Cleaner:\n\n```python\nimport importlib.util, sys\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"baseline_cache\", \"/dev/shm/baseline/cache.py\")\nm = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec); spec.loader.exec_module(m)\nfrom cache import TTLCache  # new one? \n```\nHmm, for baseline check I want to run key assertions with baseline's TTLCache and confirm they fail. Since the assertions would raise, wrap each in try/except.\n\nI'll write /dev/shm/check_baseline.py that loads baseline module and checks the four core requirements, printing which fail.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Performance is solid and O(1) amortized. Now let me confirm my tests actually discriminate — the baseline should fail these checks."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"[CALL_00029]","name":"write_file","input":{"content":"import importlib.util\n\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"baseline_cache\", \"/dev/shm/baseline/cache.py\")\nm = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\nspec.loader.exec_module(m)\nTTLCache = m.TTLCache\n\n\ndef check(name, fn):\n    try:\n        fn()\n        print(\"BASELINE PASS:\", name)\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(\"BASELINE FAIL:\", name, \"->\", type(e).__name__, e)\n\n\ndef lru():\n    now = [0.0]\n    c = TTLCache(2, 100, lambda: now[0])\n    c.put(\"a\", 1); c.put(\"b\", 2); c.get(\"a\"); c.put(\"c\", 3)\n    assert c.get(\"b\") is None, \"b should be evicted\"\n\n\ndef boundary():\n    now = [0.0]\n    c = TTLCache(5, 10, lambda: now[0])\n    c.put(\"a\", 1)\n    now[0] = 10.0\n    assert c.get(\"a\") is None, \"age == ttl must be expired\"\n\n\ndef cap_zero():\n    c = TTLCache(0, 10, lambda: 0.0)\n    c.put(\"a\", 1)\n    assert c.get(\"a\") is None\n\n\ndef update_refresh():\n    now = [0.0]\n    c = TTLCache(2, 10, lambda: now[0])\n    c.put(\"a\", 1); c.put(\"b\", 2); now[0] = 9.0; c.put(\"a\", 11)\n    now[0] = 17.0\n    assert c.get(\"a\") == 11, \"update should refresh expiry\"\n    assert c.get(\"b\") is None, \"b expired and should not linger\"\n\n\ncheck(\"true LRU eviction\", lru)\ncheck(\"ttl boundary age==ttl expired\", boundary)\ncheck(\"capacity<=0 stores nothing\", cap_zero)\ncheck(\"put update refreshes expiry and recency\", update_refresh)\n","file_path":"/dev/shm/check_baseline.py"}}]}}
