{"type":"event","event":{"type":"thinking_end","text":"Wait, my update_refresh check: after put a@0, b@0 (cap 2), at t=9 put a=11 → baseline: len(data)=2>=2 → pops first (a), then inserts a → data {b, a}? Actually baseline `put` doesn't remove existing key first; it evicts then sets `data[k]`, so updating a at capacity evicts a itself (insertion order first is a), leaving {b, a=11}. Then at t=17: get a → age 8 <10 → 11. Passes? Hmm. Let me think of a check that baseline fails for update semantics. The point: baseline's put update evicts the LRU (which happens to be a itself since insertion order). Hard to demonstrate simply. Maybe: put a@0, put b@0, get b (no effect in baseline), at t=5 put a=11 → baseline evicts a (first in insertion order) then reinserts → {b, a=11}; then get b → b hasn't expired (5<10) → returns 2. My check expected b expired at 17: b age 17 → baseline get(b): 17>10 → returns None (strict >). Passes.\n\nSo the update_refresh check might pass on baseline. That's fine—the other three discriminate. But let me make a check where baseline fails update recency: capacity 2, put a@0, put b@1, put a@2 update → true LRU order should be b, a; put c@3 → evict b. Baseline: put a@2 when len=2 → evicts first insertion order a (stale), reinsert a → order {b, a}; put c@3 → evicts b. Same result. Hmm, baseline insertion-order put actually acts LRU-ish for pure insertion workloads without gets. The differing case is gets: baseline never moves on get. So LRU check covers it.\n\nAnother baseline failure: get does not refresh recency: cap2, put a, put b, get a, put c → baseline evicts a (insertion first), so get b returns 2 and get a None → my lru() check catches it.\n\nAlso baseline fails \"expired not consume capacity\"? Consider cap1: put a@0; t=20 put b: baseline len=1>=1 → pop a, insert b → works. What about the tricky ordering? cap1 can't have reordering. cap2: put a@0, put b@3, get a@5 (baseline get doesn't move), t=11 put c: baseline evicts a (first) → {b,c}; a was expired anyway. To demonstrate baseline failure on expired-not-consuming-capacity you'd need expired behind live, which needs get-refresh — which baseline doesn't do. So that requirement is mainly about correctness with our new get behavior: since get now moves entries, expired entries can be behind live ones, so our purge must handle it. My test_expired_behind_live_in_lru_order covers exactly that, and it would pass on baseline for the wrong reason (baseline returns b since b not evicted... wait baseline: cap2, put a@0, put b@3, get a@5 (no-op, returns 1), t=11 put c → len 2>=2 evict a → {b,c}; get c=3, get b: age 8<10 → 2, get a→None. Passes baseline too). That's fine; the test exists to catch a regression in *my* implementation (e.g., if I purged from LRU front only, I'd evict b and keep expired a, failing that test). Good.\n\nNow, one more check: does the \"get does not refresh expiry\" test discriminate baseline? cap1, ttl10, put a@0, get a@9 → baseline returns 1; t=9.999 → 1; t=10.0 → baseline: 10 > 10 false → returns 1, expected None → fails. Good.\n\nLet me run the baseline checker."}}
