{"type":"event","event":{"type":"thinking_end","text":"Baseline fails 3 of 4 (the 4th happens to pass by coincidence of insertion-order eviction). My implementation passes all.\n\nNow let me review the final implementation once more, check the working tree is clean except cache.py, and clean up scratch files in /dev/shm (they're outside the repo, but tidy anyway — actually /dev/shm is RAM, no need, but I'll remove to be clean).\n\nLet me also do a final review of cache.py and git diff.\n\nOne more consideration: hidden tests might check `get` with a `default` that's mutable or check that `get` when expired returns the exact default. Ours returns `default` object as-is. Good.\n\nAlso possible hidden test: `c = TTLCache(2, 10)` using real time, quick operations → fine.\n\nPotential hidden test: put same key twice should not increase size. Ours: remove then insert → size stable. Good.\n\nPotential hidden test: capacity 0 with pre-existing... impossible.\n\nPotential hidden test: `get` on missing key doesn't affect LRU. Ours doesn't.\n\nPotential hidden test: `put` of a key when at capacity should evict LRU *after* update handling. Our remove-existing first avoids evicting when updating existing key at capacity. Correct semantics (\"Updating a key refreshes expiry and recency\" — an update shouldn't evict anything).\n\nWait, one subtle case: capacity=1, put a@0, put a@1 → data {a:1}, no eviction. Correct.\n\nAnother subtle case: at capacity, put a new key where an expired key exists whose expiry-order front is live? Can't happen: expiry order front = earliest expiry; if any expired key exists, the earliest-expiry key is expired, so purge removes at least it. All expired keys are at the front of the expiry order (since expiry times sorted). So purge removes all expired ones before hitting a live one. Good.\n\nActually, let me double check the sortedness invariant with an update: put a@0, put b@5 → expiries {a:0, b:5}. put a@10 → remove a, insert → {b:5, a:10}. Sorted. Yes, because reinsertion appends the largest timestamp (monotonic clock).\n\nWhat about `get` not touching expiries → order stays put-sorted. Eviction removes arbitrary middle? No—eviction removes oldest LRU from data, and removes that key from expiries wherever it is. Removing a middle element preserves order. Yes.\n\nI'm confident. Let me view final file, clean git status, remove scratch."}}
